1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,301
In a tug of war, the stronger
side pulls the rope its way.

2
00:00:04,301 --> 00:00:07,788
At a shared pizza, the bigger
eater takes more slices.

3
00:00:07,788 --> 00:00:10,349
In a game, every player gets a score.

4
00:00:10,749 --> 00:00:12,808
Chemistry gives each atom a score too.

5
00:00:12,808 --> 00:00:14,827
It tells us who pulled the electrons.

6
00:00:15,277 --> 00:00:19,679
Diya sits at her study table, looking
at a water molecule in her book.

7
00:00:20,029 --> 00:00:22,443
Oxygen and hydrogen share their electrons.

8
00:00:22,443 --> 00:00:24,224
Nobody gives them away fully.

9
00:00:24,724 --> 00:00:26,852
So who lost electrons, and who gained?

10
00:00:26,852 --> 00:00:29,483
By the end, you will
score any atom in seconds.

11
00:00:30,133 --> 00:00:31,792
Here is the problem.

12
00:00:31,792 --> 00:00:36,414
In sodium chloride, sodium truly
gives one electron to chlorine.

13
00:00:36,414 --> 00:00:37,672
Easy to track.

14
00:00:38,022 --> 00:00:40,297
But in water, electrons are only shared.

15
00:00:40,297 --> 00:00:41,972
Oxygen is more electronegative.

16
00:00:41,972 --> 00:00:44,328
That means it pulls
shared electrons harder.

17
00:00:44,728 --> 00:00:48,334
We need a way to track electrons
even when they are only shared.

18
00:00:48,334 --> 00:00:50,260
That way is the oxidation number.

19
00:00:50,860 --> 00:00:53,308
Let's score water on our molecule bench.

20
00:00:53,308 --> 00:00:56,952
Each oxygen hydrogen bond holds
one shared pair of electrons.

21
00:00:57,302 --> 00:00:58,305
Now pretend.

22
00:00:58,305 --> 00:01:02,336
Hand each shared pair fully to
the stronger puller, oxygen.

23
00:01:02,736 --> 00:01:04,858
Oxygen now has two extra electrons.

24
00:01:04,858 --> 00:01:06,386
Its score is minus two.

25
00:01:06,786 --> 00:01:09,317
Each hydrogen lost its one electron.

26
00:01:09,317 --> 00:01:11,050
Its score is plus one.

27
00:01:11,450 --> 00:01:12,421
Add them up.

28
00:01:12,421 --> 00:01:14,599
Minus two, plus one, plus one.

29
00:01:14,599 --> 00:01:15,499
Zero.

30
00:01:15,127 --> 00:01:17,748
Just right for a molecule with no charge.

31
00:01:18,348 --> 00:01:20,639
This score is the oxidation number.

32
00:01:20,639 --> 00:01:25,504
It is the charge an atom would have, if
every shared pair went to the stronger

33
00:01:25,504 --> 00:01:26,404
puller.

34
00:01:26,494 --> 00:01:29,044
It is a pretend charge,
a bookkeeping score.

35
00:01:29,044 --> 00:01:31,290
But it lets us track electrons anywhere.

36
00:01:31,690 --> 00:01:33,396
Now redox becomes easy.

37
00:01:33,396 --> 00:01:36,433
Oxidation means the
oxidation number goes up.

38
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:38,512
Reduction means it goes down.

39
00:01:39,162 --> 00:01:40,705
Now the main rules.

40
00:01:40,705 --> 00:01:41,861
Step by step.

41
00:01:42,211 --> 00:01:43,111
Rule one.

42
00:01:43,107 --> 00:01:45,697
An element on its own scores zero.

43
00:01:45,697 --> 00:01:49,281
Like hydrogen gas, oxygen
gas, or sodium metal.

44
00:01:50,031 --> 00:01:50,931
Rule two.

45
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:53,102
A simple ion scores its charge.

46
00:01:53,102 --> 00:01:54,906
Sodium plus is plus one.

47
00:01:54,906 --> 00:01:56,544
Chloride is minus one.

48
00:01:57,294 --> 00:01:58,194
Rule three.

49
00:01:58,112 --> 00:02:00,636
All the scores in a molecule add to zero.

50
00:02:00,636 --> 00:02:03,159
In an ion, they add to the ion's charge.

51
00:02:03,909 --> 00:02:06,174
Then a few fixed scores to remember.

52
00:02:06,524 --> 00:02:08,554
Fluorine is always minus one.

53
00:02:08,554 --> 00:02:10,696
Group one metals are plus one.

54
00:02:10,696 --> 00:02:12,839
Group two metals are plus two.

55
00:02:13,239 --> 00:02:18,242
Hydrogen is plus one, but minus one
when joined to metals, like in sodium

56
00:02:18,242 --> 00:02:19,142
hydride.

57
00:02:19,391 --> 00:02:20,639
Oxygen is minus two.

58
00:02:20,639 --> 00:02:24,557
But it is minus one in peroxides,
minus half in superoxides, and positive

59
00:02:24,557 --> 00:02:25,972
when joined to fluorine.

60
00:02:26,572 --> 00:02:29,134
Now a naming trick called Stock notation.

61
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:31,753
Some metals can have more than one score.

62
00:02:32,103 --> 00:02:34,147
Iron can be plus two or plus three.

63
00:02:34,147 --> 00:02:38,003
So we write its score in Roman
numerals, in brackets, after its name.

64
00:02:38,403 --> 00:02:40,460
F e O is iron two oxide.

65
00:02:40,460 --> 00:02:43,309
F e two O three is iron three oxide.

66
00:02:43,309 --> 00:02:45,575
The name tells you the score.

67
00:02:46,175 --> 00:02:49,302
Now compare oxygen in two
molecules side by side.

68
00:02:49,302 --> 00:02:51,272
Water, and hydrogen peroxide.

69
00:02:51,672 --> 00:02:54,101
In water, one oxygen holds two hydrogens.

70
00:02:54,101 --> 00:02:57,460
In hydrogen peroxide, two
oxygens hold hands in the middle.

71
00:02:57,860 --> 00:02:59,177
Pause and think.

72
00:02:59,177 --> 00:03:03,299
What is the oxidation number
of oxygen in hydrogen peroxide?

73
00:03:06,499 --> 00:03:07,399
Minus one!

74
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:08,935
The two hydrogens give plus two.

75
00:03:08,935 --> 00:03:11,450
Split between two oxygens,
each gets minus one.

76
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:14,092
The pair between the two
oxygens is shared equally.

77
00:03:14,742 --> 00:03:15,878
Remember this.

78
00:03:15,878 --> 00:03:17,833
Score goes up, oxidation.

79
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:19,894
Score goes down, reduction.

80
00:03:20,594 --> 00:03:22,285
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:22,585 --> 00:03:27,413
Find the oxidation number of chromium
in potassium dichromate, K two C

82
00:03:27,413 --> 00:03:28,728
r two O seven.

83
00:03:29,228 --> 00:03:30,639
Potassium is plus one.

84
00:03:30,639 --> 00:03:32,414
Two potassiums give plus two.

85
00:03:33,164 --> 00:03:34,635
Oxygen is minus two.

86
00:03:34,635 --> 00:03:36,855
Seven oxygens give minus fourteen.

87
00:03:37,605 --> 00:03:39,558
Two chromiums give two x.

88
00:03:39,558 --> 00:03:41,391
Everything adds to zero.

89
00:03:41,391 --> 00:03:45,284
Plus two, plus two x, minus
fourteen, equals zero.

90
00:03:46,034 --> 00:03:48,969
So two x is twelve, and x is plus six.

91
00:03:48,969 --> 00:03:51,410
Chromium is in the plus six state.

92
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:54,017
Now spot redox with scores.

93
00:03:54,017 --> 00:03:58,287
In copper oxide plus hydrogen,
copper drops from plus two to zero.

94
00:03:58,287 --> 00:03:59,187
Reduced.

95
00:03:58,981 --> 00:04:01,444
Hydrogen rises from zero to plus one.

96
00:04:01,444 --> 00:04:02,344
Oxidised.

97
00:04:02,836 --> 00:04:04,298
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:04,648 --> 00:04:07,226
Students give oxygen minus two everywhere.

99
00:04:07,226 --> 00:04:10,298
In hydrogen peroxide,
that gives a wrong answer.

100
00:04:10,698 --> 00:04:11,631
Check first.

101
00:04:11,631 --> 00:04:13,950
In peroxides, oxygen is minus one.

102
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:16,355
With fluorine, oxygen turns positive.

103
00:04:17,005 --> 00:04:18,503
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:18,503 --> 00:04:22,582
Boards ask you to find oxidation
numbers and write Stock names.

105
00:04:22,582 --> 00:04:26,823
Entrance tests hide a peroxide or
an odd ion inside the question.

106
00:04:27,423 --> 00:04:28,719
Exam level now.

107
00:04:28,719 --> 00:04:33,576
What is the oxidation number of
manganese in potassium manganate, K two

108
00:04:33,576 --> 00:04:34,719
M n O four?

109
00:04:34,719 --> 00:04:36,070
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:42,990
The answer is B.

111
00:04:42,990 --> 00:04:43,956
Plus six.

112
00:04:44,456 --> 00:04:46,007
Two potassiums give plus two.

113
00:04:46,007 --> 00:04:47,597
Four oxygens give minus eight.

114
00:04:48,097 --> 00:04:51,150
Plus two, plus x, minus
eight, equals zero.

115
00:04:51,150 --> 00:04:52,494
So x is plus six.

116
00:04:52,994 --> 00:04:54,339
A is plus seven.

117
00:04:54,339 --> 00:04:59,532
That belongs to potassium permanganate,
KM n O four, a different compound.

118
00:05:00,132 --> 00:05:01,672
Here is your recap.

119
00:05:01,672 --> 00:05:03,255
Screenshot this card.

120
00:05:06,455 --> 00:05:08,641
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

121
00:05:09,041 --> 00:05:09,941
One.

122
00:05:09,506 --> 00:05:12,458
What is the oxidation
number of any free element?

123
00:05:13,258 --> 00:05:14,158
Two.

124
00:05:13,750 --> 00:05:16,389
Name F e two O three in Stock notation.

125
00:05:17,189 --> 00:05:18,089
Three.

126
00:05:17,775 --> 00:05:21,350
Find the oxidation number
of sulphur in H two SO four.

127
00:05:22,250 --> 00:05:25,021
Next time: how do we sort
redox reactions into families?

128
00:05:25,021 --> 00:05:27,039
We find out in types of redox reactions.
